You are a senior full-stack engineer. Build a production-quality School Classroom Management web application from scratch. Work step by step: first propose the plan and schema, then generate complete, runnable code for every file, with no placeholders or "TODO" comments.

## 1. Goal
A school web app where admins create classrooms and assign teachers and students. Teachers sign in and see a dashboard of their classrooms across terms, open a classroom to see its students, and open a student to see full details.

## 2. Tech Stack (fixed, do not change)
- Backend: Spring Boot 4.1.0, Java 26.0.2, Maven
  - Spring Web, Spring Data JPA (Hibernate), Spring Security (JWT access + refresh tokens, BCrypt), Spring Validation, Flyway for migrations, Lombok, springdoc-openapi (Swagger UI)
- Frontend: Angular 22.1.2
  - Standalone components, signals, lazy-loaded routes, functional route guards, HTTP interceptors, reactive forms
  - UI: Angular Material (or Tailwind CSS), responsive and accessible
- Database: PostgreSQL 18.6
- Dev setup: Docker Compose (postgres, backend, frontend), .env.example
- Testing: JUnit 5 + Mockito + Testcontainers (backend), Jasmine/Karma or Jest (frontend)

## 3. User Roles
- ADMIN: manages terms, classrooms, teachers, students, and assignments
- TEACHER: read-only access to their own classrooms and the students in them
- Teachers must NOT access classrooms or students they are not assigned to. Enforce this on the backend (method security / service-level checks), not just in the UI.

## 4. Core Requirements
1. Terms: Admin creates terms (name, startDate, endDate). The system computes each term's status as PAST, CURRENT, or FUTURE from today's date.
2. Classrooms: Admin can create, edit, and delete classrooms (name, grade/section, subject, room number, term).
3. Teacher assignment: Each classroom has one or more teachers. One teacher can be assigned to many classrooms (many-to-many).
4. Student assignment: Each classroom has one or more students (many-to-many). A student can be in multiple classrooms across terms.
5. Teacher login: After sign-in, the teacher lands on the Teacher Dashboard.
6. Teacher Dashboard:
   - Shows all of the teacher's classrooms grouped into: Current Term, Previous Terms, Future Terms
   - Future Terms section appears only if the teacher has classrooms assigned in future terms
   - Each classroom card shows name, subject, term, and student count
7. Classroom page: Clicking a classroom shows a searchable, sortable, paginated list of its students (name, roll number, grade).
8. Student detail page: Clicking a student shows full details (name, roll number, DOB, gender, contact info, guardian info, address, enrollment date, classrooms they are in, notes).

## 5. Database Design
Normalized PostgreSQL schema with primary keys, foreign keys, unique constraints, indexes, and created_at/updated_at timestamps:
users, teachers, students, terms, classrooms, classroom_teachers, classroom_students.
Provide JPA entities and Flyway migration scripts (V1__init.sql, etc.).

## 6. API Design
RESTful endpoints with DTOs (Java records), Bean Validation, global @RestControllerAdvice exception handling, and proper HTTP status codes:
- POST /api/auth/login, POST /api/auth/refresh, GET /api/auth/me
- Admin CRUD: /api/terms, /api/classrooms, /api/teachers, /api/students
- POST/DELETE /api/classrooms/{id}/teachers and /api/classrooms/{id}/students
- GET /api/teacher/dashboard (classrooms grouped into current, previous, future)
- GET /api/teacher/classrooms/{id}/students
- GET /api/teacher/students/{id}
Include pagination (Pageable), sorting, and role-based authorization (@PreAuthorize).

## 7. Frontend Pages
- Login page
- Teacher: Dashboard, Classroom Students page, Student Detail page
- Admin: manage terms, classrooms, teachers, students, and assignments
- Auth service, JWT interceptor with token refresh, role guards, loading/error/empty states, toast notifications
- Clean, responsive UI (mobile + desktop)

## 8. Non-Functional Requirements
- Security: input validation, CORS config, login rate limiting, no secrets in code, externalized config (application.yml + env vars)
- Clean, simple, well-commented code with clear package structure (controller, service, repository, entity, dto, config, security)
- Seed data (CommandLineRunner or Flyway): 1 admin, 3 teachers, 30 students, 6 classrooms across past, current, and future terms
- Unit/integration tests for term status logic and teacher access control

